  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   Central idea: Utilize multi-start global optimization coupled with local refinement to escape local optima and find the global minimum for the non-convex Erds minimum overlap problem. The approach combines evolutionary strategies for broad exploration with gradient-based local optimization for precise refinement.\n   Bottleneck addressed: Current method is prone to local optima, particularly when heuristic parameters like mutation rates are fixed. This approach adapts search strategies based on convergence patterns and incorporates uncertainty-aware sampling.\n   Justification: Non-convex optimization requires a balance between exploration and exploitation. Polynomial-time EA guarantees improvement, while adaptive local refinement prevents premature convergence.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Objective: min \u2225C_max; where C_max = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641; \u2211h[i]dx=1\n   - Representation: 350-point step function discretized over [0,2]\n   - Optimization: Divide into two stages:\n     1. Global exploration (EA) using bisecting tournament selection with adaptive bounds\n     2. Local refinement (gradient-based) with projected gradients\n   - Solver adaptation: Use NLopt's LD_MMA for constraint handling specifically across gradient-projection interface\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   on_start: Validate initial_h_values then form initial population through:\n     - Mutation-based evolution using exponential crossover\n     - Repair phase: Box-constrained projection preserving mass conservation\n   core_loop:\n     Phase 1: Global optimization\n        for iteration:\n          1. Sample multiple starts via adaptive mutation\n          2. Trim population based on fitness-ranking histogram\n          3. Evaluate using FFT-based cross-correlation\n          4. Update global best if improvement triggered\n     Phase 2: Local refinement\n        for perturbation:\n          1. Inject adversarial uncertainty via multiplicative noise\n          2. Smooth using NLopt with dynamics-aware constraints\n          3. Update gradients in projected space\n     Termination: ~1000 evaluations or \u221d150 mutations\n\n4. Search choices\n   - Primary variants: \n     \u25cb Mutation: Cauchy-form vs. sigmoid-form\n     \u25cb Selection: Binary tournament vs. ranked tournament\n     \u25cb Learning: Lamarckian inheritance vs. Weitbachtard derivatives\n   - Parameter schedules:\n     \u2022 Mutation \u03c9 \u2208 [0.3, 0.8] scaling with evaluations\n     \u2022 Crossover \u03b1 \u2208 {logistic(0.5), tanh(0.3)}\n     \u2022 Population pruning \u03b2 \u2265 65% of top tier\n\n5. Compute budget\n   - Operation lx: 350-point FFT = \u221d 7\u00d710\u2074 operations\n   - EA phase: 500 iterations \u00d7 2 populations = \u221d 2 million evals\n   - Local refinement: 20 calls \u00d7 100ms for gradient projection\n   - Protection: Implement timed checkpointing every 20 iterations\n\n6. Validation\n   - Post-processing: \n     \u220e Verify 0\u2264h\u22641 vector-wise\n     \u220e Recheck constraint conservation via \u2211h[i]dx=1\n     \u220e Run gradient descent verification on minima\n   - Benchmark: Check against 0.38\u207b baseline through correlation tensors",
